KUBERNETES SERVICES (FROM ZERO)
First: what problem do Services solve?

Pods:

Are ephemeral

Have changing IPs

Can be recreated anytime

👉 You cannot reliably talk to pods directly.

So Kubernetes introduces Services.

🧱 WHAT IS A SERVICE? (VERY BASIC)
Simple definition

A Service is:

A stable network endpoint that points to a set of pods.

It gives you:

Stable IP

Stable DNS name

Load balancing

🧠 Key idea (MEMORIZE)

Pods change. Services don’t.

🧩 HOW A SERVICE WORKS (REAL FLOW)

You create pods with labels

You create a Service with a selector

Kubernetes:

Finds matching pods

Creates Endpoints

Routes traffic to pods

🔍 See this live
kubectl get svc
kubectl get endpoints


Endpoints = actual pod IPs behind the service.

🧪 HANDS-ON SETUP (DO THIS)
Step 1️⃣ Create a deployment
kubectl create deployment web --image=nginx
kubectl scale deployment web --replicas=3


Check:

kubectl get pods -o wide


Notice:

3 pods

3 different IPs

Step 2️⃣ Expose it as a Service (ClusterIP)
kubectl expose deployment web --port=80 --target-port=80


Check:

kubectl get svc


You’ll see:

TYPE: ClusterIP
CLUSTER-IP: 10.x.x.x

Step 3️⃣ Verify endpoints
kubectl get endpoints web


You’ll see:

IP1:80, IP2:80, IP3:80


👉 Service → Endpoints → Pods

🧠 SERVICE TYPES (VERY IMPORTANT)
1️⃣ ClusterIP (default)
What it is

Internal-only service

Accessible inside cluster

Used for

Backend services

Microservice-to-microservice communication

Failure modes
Problem	Look at
Service not reachable	Endpoints
No traffic	Selector mismatch
DNS fails	CoreDNS

Debug:

kubectl describe svc
kubectl get endpoints

2️⃣ NodePort
What it is

Exposes service on node IP + port

Port range: 30000–32767

Example:

NodeIP:30080

Used for

Quick testing

Simple external access

Failure modes
Problem	Look at
Can’t access	Node firewall
Traffic uneven	kube-proxy

Debug:

kubectl get svc
kubectl describe svc

3️⃣ LoadBalancer (cloud only)
What it is

Creates a cloud load balancer

Routes traffic → nodes → pods

Used for

Production external access

In EKS:

Creates AWS ELB / ALB

Failure modes
Problem	Look at
LB not created	Cloud controller
No traffic	Security groups
503 errors	Pod readiness

Debug:

kubectl describe svc

🔥 MOST COMMON SERVICE FAILURES (REAL LIFE)
❌ Service exists but no traffic
Cause

Selector doesn’t match pod labels.

Check:

kubectl get svc -o yaml
kubectl get pods --show-labels

❌ Service endpoints empty
Meaning

Service found zero pods

Debug:

kubectl get endpoints


If empty → labels mismatch or pods not Ready.

❌ Pods running but service not reachable
Common reasons

Readiness probe failing

kube-proxy issue

NetworkPolicy blocking

Debug:

kubectl describe pod
kubectl get endpoints

🧠 WHO IS RESPONSIBLE? (IMPORTANT)
Issue	Look at
No endpoints	Controller
Traffic not routing	kube-proxy
DNS not resolving	CoreDNS
External access fails	LB / cloud
Pod not in service	Readiness probe
🧠 DEBUGGING FLOW FOR SERVICES (MEMORIZE)

When service fails:

Does service exist?

kubectl get svc


Does it have endpoints?

kubectl get endpoints


Are pods Ready?

kubectl describe pod


Is DNS resolving?

nslookup service-name


Never jump steps.

🧠 ANALOGY (STICKY)

Pods = shop workers

Service = shop phone number

Workers change → phone number stays

✅ CHECKPOINT QUESTIONS (ANSWER MENTALLY)

Can a service exist without pods? → Yes

Can pods exist without a service? → Yes

Does service load balance? → Yes

Does service run containers? → No

If these feel easy → you’re progressing fast.

